{{smaller block|Now Cap'n Cyrus is the luckiest seaman that ever sat on a ''thwart.'' He never had nothin' happen to him.
{{right|''F. R. Stockton'', Merry Chanter, iii.}}}}

{{smaller|'''After-thwart''', the thwart furthest aft in a whale-boat, occupied by the after-oarsman. Also called ''stroke-thwart.''—'''Bow-thwart''', the second thwart in a whale-boat, occupied by the bow-oarsman.}}

'''thwartedly''' (thwâr′ted-li), ''adv.'' Athwart; obliquely. [Rare.]

{{smaller block|We do not live in the inside of a pearl; but in an atmosphere through which a burning sun shines ''thwartedly'', and over which a sorrowful night must far prevail.
{{right|''Ruskin'', Lectures on Art, § 176.}}}}

'''thwarter''' (thwâr′tėr), ''n.'' [< ''thwart''<sup>1</sup> + ''-er''<sup>1</sup>.] One who or that which thwarts or crosses.

'''thwarter-ill''' (thwâr′tėr-il), ''n.'' Same as ''louping-ill.''

'''thwart-hawse''' (thwârt′hâz), ''adv.'' ''Naut.'', across the hawse.

'''thwarting''' (thwâr′ting), ''n.'' [Verbal n. of ''thwart''<sup>1</sup>, ''v.''] Opposing act or action; whatever frustrates or baffles or tends to defeat one's purposes, wishes, designs, etc.

{{smaller block|The woman is of such disposition that in the ende of thirtie yeeres marriage there shal euery day be found ''thwartings'' in her condition, and alteration in her conuersation
{{right|''Guevara'', Letters (tr. by Hellowes, 1577), p. 306.}}}}

{{smaller block|The ''thwartings'' of your dispositions.
{{right|''Shak.'', Cor., iii. 2. 21.}}}}

'''thwarting''' (thwâr′ting), ''p. a.'' [Ppr. of ''thwart''<sup>1</sup>.] Perverse; contrary.

{{smaller block|Such shields tooke the name Clypei, i. chased and engraven, not in the old word in Latine Cluere, which signi-}}

