¶
'''thwartingly''' (thwâr′ting-li), ''adv.'' Perversely; in an opposing or baffling manner.

{{smaller block|It is wittingly observed that the over-precise are so ''thwartingly'' cross to the superstitious in all things that they will scarce do a good work because a heretic doth it.
{{right|''Rev. T. Adams'', Works, II. 407.}}}}

'''thwartly''' (thwârt′li), ''adv.'' [< ''thwart''<sup>1</sup> + ''-ly''<sup>2</sup>.] In a contrary manner; with opposition; perversely.

{{smaller block|Sith man then in judgeinge so ''thwartly'' is bente
To satisfie fansie, and not true intente.
{{right|''W. Kethe'' (1554). (''Davies.'')}}}}

'''thwartness''' (thwârt′nes), ''n.'' [< ''thwart''<sup>1</sup> + ''-ness.''] The state or quality of being contrary; untowardness; perverseness.

{{smaller block|Can any man . . . defend it lawfull, upon some unkind usages, or ''thwartness'' of disposition, for a parent to abandon and forsake his child, or the son to cast off his parent?
{{right|''Bp. Hall'', Cases of Conscience, iv. 2.}}}}

'''thwartover†''', ''a.'' [< ME. ''thwert over''; < ''thwart''<sup>1</sup> + ''over.''] Contrary; baffling.

{{smaller block|And for fifteene long dayes and nights the ''thwartover'' and crosse north easterly winde blew us nothing but lengthening of our sorrowes. ''John Taylor'', Works (1630). (''Nares.'')}}

'''thwartship''' (thwârt′ship), ''a.'' [< ''thwart''<sup>1</sup>, ''prep.'', + ''ship.''] ''Naut.'', lying across the vessel.

'''thwartships''' (thwârt′ships), ''adv.'' [< ''thwart''<sup>1</sup>, ''prep.'', 1, + ''ship'' + adv. gen. ''-s.''] ''Naut.'', across the ship from side to side: opposed to ''fore and''

