'''thwite†''', ''v. t.'' [< ME. ''thwiten'', ''thwyten'', < AS. ''thwitan'', cut. Hence the var. ''white''<sup>2</sup>, and ult. the deriv. ''thwittle'', var. ''whittle'', and ''thwaite''<sup>1</sup>.] To cut; whittle. [Obsolete or prov. Eng.]

{{smaller block|Twigges fallow, rede,
And grene eek, and som weren whyte,
Swiche as men to these cages ''thwyte'',
