{{right|''Chaucer'', House of Fame, l. 1938.}}}}

{{smaller block|It [the bow] was peynted wel and ''thwitten'' [var. ''twhitten'', ''twythen''].
{{right|''Rom. of the Rose'', l. 933.}}}}

'''thwittle†''', ''n.'' [< ME. ''thwitel'', a knife, < ''thwiten'', cut: see ''thwite''.] A whittle; a knife.

{{smaller block|A Sheffeld ''thwitel'' baar he in his hose.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Reeve's Tale, l. 13.}}}}

'''thwittle†''', ''v. t.'' [< ''thwittle'', ''n.'', or freq. of ''thwite''.] To whittle.

'''thworl''' (thėrl or thwôrl), ''n.'' A variant of ''whorl''.

'''thy''' (ᴛ̵ʜī), ''pron.'' [< ME. ''thy'', ''thi'', a shortened form of ''thin'', < AS. ''thin'': see ''thine''. The -''n'' was dropped as being appar. a mere inflectional ending. Cf. ''my''.] Of or pertaining to thee: possessive of the pronoun ''thou'', second person singular. It is used in solemn and grave style. See ''thine''.

{{smaller block|For beetinge was ''thi'' bodi blewe.
{{right|''Hymns to Virgin'', etc. (E. E. T. S.), p. 13}}}}

{{smaller block|Good ''thy'' judgement, wench;
''Thy'' bright elections cleere.
{{right|''Marston'', Antonio and Mellida, I., i. 1.}}}}

{{smaller block|These are ''thy'' glorious works, Parent of good.
{{right|''Milton'', P. L., v. 153.}}}}

